<section class="cs-exec">
  <div class="cs-exec-inner">
    <p class="cs-exec-body">When a product catalog is organized around SKU codes and warehouse identifiers, it reflects how a business manages inventory, not how customers discover products. In mobile accessories, purchase intent is highly specific: phone model first, finish second, brand third. The gap between internal naming conventions and search behavior has a direct cost: missed impressions, reduced bid eligibility, and Shopping products competing for queries they were never designed to serve.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">This account had built its campaign structure the same way it built its catalog: incrementally. Each time a new product line launched or a new type of coverage was needed, another campaign was added. That approach was logical at each individual step. Over years, it produced an account with more than twenty active campaigns and no clearly defined boundaries between them.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">Twenty-plus campaigns isn't comprehensive coverage. It's evidence that the account was never designed — it just grew.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">At $1.6M in managed spend, the consequences of that accumulated complexity were no longer easy to absorb.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">When we reviewed this account, the campaign list told the story immediately: more than 20 campaigns, limited hierarchy, and several campaign types eligible for the same demand. The client had been adding campaigns every time they wanted coverage in a new area, which made sense at the time. But over years, it meant brand queries were triggering general campaigns, Shopping and Performance Max were both eligible for the same product searches, and there were no defined boundaries between them. The feed had the same problem: built when they first connected Shopify, the product titles still read as SKU codes and warehouse references. Not what buyers search for. We rebuilt the feed first, then the campaign structure, then the bidding signals. Seven months later: 4.14× blended ROAS, $6.6M in tracked revenue.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- MODULE 7: THE CHALLENGE -->
<section class="cs-challenge">
  <div class="cs-challenge-inner">
    <h2 class="cs-h2">Years of campaign growth had created overlap. The product feed was limiting relevance.</h2>
    <p class="cs-para">The client had grown this account organically over years. Every time they wanted more coverage, they added a campaign. By the time we got in, there were 20+ running simultaneously with no negative keyword walls between them, which meant multiple Shopping, Search, and Performance Max campaigns were eligible for overlapping areas of demand.</p>
    <p class="cs-para">The feed was the other problem. It was built when they first connected Shopify, and the titles were still SKU codes and internal model references. Not what buyers search for. Google was showing their products on the wrong queries. They were visible where intent was low, missing where it was high. Blended ROAS for the prior ten months: 2.59× on $1.6M spend.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- MODULE 8: WHAT WE FOUND -->
<section class="cs-findings">
  <div class="cs-findings-inner">
    <h2 class="cs-h2">What we found when we got into the account</h2>

    <div class="cs-findings-list" role="list">
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">01</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">Product titles written for the warehouse, not the buyer</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">SKU codes, model references, internal naming: none of it matched what buyers type into Google. This was determining which queries the products showed for, and the match was wrong. High-intent searches weren't triggering the right products. Lower-intent queries were.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">02</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">Multiple campaign types eligible for the same demand</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">With 20+ campaigns and no defined query boundaries between them, multiple Shopping, Search, and Performance Max campaigns were eligible for overlapping areas of demand. Budget was being distributed without a clear signal about which campaign was the right fit for each query type.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">03</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">Performance Max launched without audience signals</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">PMax had no Customer Match data connected: no purchase history, no CRM data, nothing. The algorithm was starting from scratch on every eligible query. The client had a full segmentable customer list that had never been linked to the account.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">04</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">ROAS targets set to aspirational numbers, not account history</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">The bid targets were set to what the client wanted to hit, not what the account had actually been doing. The bidding system was restricting spend to chase a number it couldn't reach, which reduced impressions, reduced conversions, and gave the algorithm less data to work with.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-mid-hook">
      <p class="cs-mid-hook-text">If you have 10+ campaigns and no one has audited the structure, there's a reasonable chance several of them are eligible for the same queries. We find it in most large accounts we take over, and it's one of the first things the Growth Diagnostic surfaces.</p>
