<!-- MODULE 10: WHAT CHANGED -->
<section class="cs-interventions">
  <div class="cs-interventions-inner">
    <h2 class="cs-h2">The order mattered: feed first, bidding last</h2>
    <p class="cs-para">Feed first. Then campaign structure. Then negative keyword lists. Then audience signals. Then bid targets. The sequence matters because each layer depends on the one before it working correctly.</p>

    <div class="cs-intervention-list">
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">01</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Rewrote every product title to match what buyers actually search for</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">We rebuilt the titles to lead with category, compatibility, and brand: "iPhone 16 Case Clear MagSafe Compatible" instead of "SKU-AJ7729-IP16-CLR." Impression share on high-intent queries moved within the first week of the feed refresh.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">02</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Consolidated 20+ campaigns around three defined roles</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">Brand Defense, Core Shopping, and Performance Max: each role with a clear job and defined query ownership. Individual campaigns were structured within those roles — what changed was the logic, not just the count. Each tier now owns its query type and the adjacent tiers are explicitly excluded.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">03</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Built negative keyword lists that kept each campaign in its lane</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">We sculpted negative keyword lists for each tier to enforce the boundaries. Each campaign now owns its query type and budget flows accordingly.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">04</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Connected the client's customer list to PMax as audience signals</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">Their CRM had never been linked to the ad account. We uploaded verified purchaser history as Customer Match signals, giving the Performance Max algorithm a real reference point for who it was looking for.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">05</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Reset bid targets to what the account had actually been doing</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">We pulled 90-day conversion baselines and set targets against those, not aspirational numbers. Once the targets were realistic, the bidding system started spending again and conversions followed.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-callout">
      <p class="cs-callout-text">The feed is the first thing we look at in any Google Shopping account. It determines which queries you show for, which products get surfaced, and how the algorithm bids. Most accounts we take over have had years of bid tuning on top of a feed that was never fixed.</p>
    </div>

